#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161789 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161997 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84c1cf7c-e334-5173-8ae4-86af0dce56ab" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Median Given the CDF a Continuous Random Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "dd626772-dd96-5840-8375-9ace79fecf70" :question/math-academy-id "e-8237" :question/problem "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n${0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 1, \\\\ 1 - \\frac{1}{x^{4}},\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 1$." :question/worked-solution "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq m) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq m) = F(m) = 1-\\dfrac{1}{m^4}, \\quad m \\geq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n1-\\dfrac{1}{m^4} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad m = \\sqrt[4]2\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because\n\n$$\nm \\geq 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161790 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161998 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a79c4207-c5d6-5386-818e-a7e532492732" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Median Given the PDF a Continuous Random Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "d22e7154-ac3c-5ac6-ad86-77a7eb08f82e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8238" :question/problem "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n${0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 1, \\\\ \\frac{2}{x^{3}},\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 1$." :question/worked-solution "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq m) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le m) &= \\int_{1}^{m}\\frac{2}{x^{3}}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.- \\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\right|_{1}^{m} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{m^{2}} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\frac{1}{m^{2}} + 1 &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{m^{2}} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nm &= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because\n\n$$\nm \\geq 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161791 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161999 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "563f7ff5-8ebb-5130-badb-f092fa348959" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quartile of a Continuous Random Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "3be25e37-7162-5565-86d2-47ea69c599ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-8239" :question/problem "Find, to three decimal places, the interquartile range of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n${0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0, \\\\ \\frac{x^{2}}{25},\\end{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 5,; 1, \\mid x > 5$." :question/worked-solution "To find the interquartile range, we first need to find the lower quartile and the upper quartile.\n\nThe lower quartile of a random variable $X$ is the number $q_1$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq q_1) = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nand the upper quartile is the number $q_3$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq q_3) = \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the lower quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le q_{1}) &= \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\nF(q_{1}) &= \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{25}q_{1}^{2} &= \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\nq_{1}^{2} &= \\frac{25}{4} \\\\\nq_{1} &= \\frac{5}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the upper quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le q_{3}) &= \\frac{3}{4} \\\\\nF(q_{3}) &= \\frac{3}{4} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{25}q_{3}^{2} &= \\frac{3}{4} \\\\\nq_{3}^{2} &= \\frac{75}{4} \\\\\nq_{3} &= \\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\nIQR = q_3 - q_1 =\\dfrac{5\\sqrt3}{2} - \\dfrac{5}{2}\\approx 1.830\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161792 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162000 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84b181c4-fc23-5a68-8042-55f02b1e5e8a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Percentile of a Continuous Random Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "d4d4f78a-05e2-57cf-8630-110daa4b61da" :question/math-academy-id "e-8240" :question/problem "Find the $27$th percentile of the random variable $X$ if the probability distribution function of $X$ is given by\n${\\frac{2}{75}x,\\begin{vmatrix}5 \\le x \\le 10, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$." :question/worked-solution "The $100p$th percentile of a random variable $X$ is the number $x_p$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq x_p) = p\n$$\n\nIn our case, we want to find the $27$th percentile, which corresponds to the decimal $p=0.27$. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le x_{0.27}) &= \\int_{5}^{x_{0.27}}\\frac{2}{75}xdx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{x^{2}}{75}\\right|_{5}^{x_{0.27}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{x_{0.27}^{2}}{75} - \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $27$th percentile is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x_{0.27}^{2}}{75} - \\frac{1}{3} &= 0.27 \\\\\nx_{0.27}^{2} &= 75(\\frac{27}{100} + \\frac{1}{3}) \\\\\nx_{0.27} &= 6.727\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161999 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "563f7ff5-8ebb-5130-badb-f092fa348959" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quartile of a Continuous Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8eb81564-9ad5-59dc-9e15-1e7c5d7a547e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (4) - \\ln (3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161139} {:answer/id #uuid "abb16d90-b8bc-5b89-a8c2-8910ef74798a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161140} {:answer/id #uuid "ddecf1d0-05b6-54d2-9db3-626fcba9cf4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln (3) - \\ln (4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161141} {:answer/id #uuid "0c5a28d5-56db-5e59-a8d4-992720b4b22b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\